### Why not use `array.sort(() => Math.random() - 0.5)`?
You will often see this one-liner online:
```javascript
// DO NOT USE THIS FOR REAL APPLICATIONS
array.sort(() => Math.random() - 0.5);
```
**Why to avoid it:**
1. **Not truly random:** It has a significant statistical bias (some outcomes are far more likely than others).
2. **Poor performance:** It takes $O(n \log n)$ time, whereas Fisher-Yates takes $O(n)$.

Here are two ways to implement Binary Search in Java: the **Iterative approach** (recommended because it is more memory efficient) and the **Recursive approach**.

> **Note:** Binary search only works on **sorted arrays**.

---

### 1. Iterative Approach (Recommended)
This approach runs in $O(\log n)$ time and takes $O(1)$ extra space.

```java
public class BinarySearch {

    public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {
        int low = 0;
        int high = arr.length - 1;

        while (low <= high) {
            // Prevents potential integer overflow compared to (low + high) / 2
            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;

            // Check if target is present at mid
            if (arr[mid] == target) {
                return mid; // Target found, return its index
            }

            // If target is greater, ignore the left half
            if (arr[mid] < target) {
                low = mid + 1;
            } 
            // If target is smaller, ignore the right half
            else {
                high = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        // Target was not present in the array
        return -1;
    }

